ojudge
======
OCaml-based competitive programming judging platform using CGI and podman.

architecture
============

web server
----------

the web server is lighttpd for CGI. This calls OCaml binaries which manage
interactivity. Dispatching containers to evaluate solutions is performed by a daemon
to ensure we 1) limit the number of concurrent executions 2) don't block the CGI processes
from returning after submissions.

daemon
------

there's a daemon that runs on the bare host, looking for submissions that haven't been evaluated 
or dispatched. It also limits concurrency to two containers at a time.

containers
----------

each submission runs in its own container, started by the dispatching daemon.

see containers/arch.txt for container design

directory structures
====================

code eval
---------

a code evaluation has the following directory structure:

├ {submission_id}
    ├── code
    │   └── main.ml
    ├── inputs
    │   ├── ex1.in
    │   ├── ex2.in
    │   └── ex3.in
    ├── outputs
    │   ├── ex1.out
    │   ├── ex2.out
    │   └── ex3.out

outputs are generated outputs, associated with the corresponding inputs.

when judging a submission the test case answers aren't in the container that compiles and runs the code.

additionally, the code and inputs directories are read only. the code because there's no reason to change it, and build is done in a temp directory, and the inputs to ensure they aren't tampered with. these are bind mounted in with the :ro permissions set. 

outputs obviously have to be writable in the container.

from within the container we have the following mount points for the above code (note: the above code is what it looks like on the server's filesystem, not in the container):

/code
/inputs
/outputs

these have 1:1 correspondences with the above directories on the server.

server filesystem
-----------------

we know what a singular code evaluation looks like on the filesystem from above, below is the rest of the server's state (there is no conventional db, only filesystem state):

| /var/lib/ojudge
    


ok, so what do we actually need?

submissions/{user_id}/{submission_id}
    /{data listed above (code, inputs, outputs, and additional metadata about execution)}
pending-review/ # once a submission is reviewed, the txt file is deleted. these files just serve as a pointer to a submissions/{submission_id} dir
    /{submission_id}.txt
problems/{problem_id}
    /{problem text}
    /{'hidden' test cases (in + out)}
contests/{contest_id}
    /{submission links (user_id/submission_id)}
    /{current status (user_id and completed problem count)}
    /{problems}/{problem_id}
